* 18 Oct 26 dm  New (terminal 140 by 42, phase 3).
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_guardmode(name, minw, minh)
*
*     name        what the program is called in the warning (IDE); null takes the lock off
*     minw minh   the size the program is laid out for
*
*   SD's own full-screen programs that read their keys through the library (the IDE and CED) are laid out for 140 by 42 and show the
*   upper left corner of the layout when the window is smaller.  So long as the cursor ($sl_curat) is in the part that shows that is
*   safe; when it is not, $sl_key does not hand the program a key but takes the keyboard itself ($sl_guard: a warning in the upper
*   left, every key dropped, Ctrl-Q the one way out) until the window is made bigger.  Ctrl-Q, then y, makes $sl_key hand back the
*   key 'guardquit' from then on (and the dialogues of the library read it as Esc), which the program turns into leaving without
*   saving.  SCREEN CLOSE and $sl_cleanup take the lock off, so it never reaches the next program; a user's SCREEN OPEN program does
*   not ask for it, so SD imposes nothing on it.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_guardmode(name, minw, minh)
$catalog $sl_guardmode

   common /slguard/ g.on, g.name, g.minw, g.minh, g.quit

   g.quit = 0
   if name = '' then
      g.on = 0
   end else
      g.on = 1
      g.name = name
      g.minw = minw
      g.minh = minh
   end

   return
end

* END-CODE
